WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
React Native UI performance has a strict upper limit when relying on a congested JavaScript thread to calculate animation frames.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
In order to maintain a fluid 60 frames per second on modern devices, we have to route around this congestion completely.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Under the legacy architecture, the asynchronous bridge created a constant bottleneck.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.000
It had to ping the heavily loaded JS thread on every single frame just to calculate the next incremental step of an animation.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:36.000
React Native's new architecture solves this by removing the bridge, replacing it with the JavaScript interface or JSI.

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.000
This animated diagram shows two pipelines, a JavaScript thread and a native UI thread, connected directly by a synchronous JSI block.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
You construct an animation graph in JavaScript exactly once, pass it across the interface, and the native thread spins it in a continuous loop.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Starting with version 0.76, the framework requires the use native driver field to be explicitly defined.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Omitting it now triggers a warning and mixing native and non-native drivers within a single parallel group results in a runtime error.

10
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Achieving fluid uninterrupted transitions means completely decoupling the animation frame execution loop from JavaScript.

11
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The foundational unit of this declarative graph is animated.value.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Animated.value is a class that holds a single mutable number.

13
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
The animation runtime engine tracks this number independently outside of standard React state.

14
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Instantiating an animated.value inside a U-state block is a common error.

15
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
As the component renders, the object is repeatedly destroyed and recreated.

16
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.000
To guarantee persistence, wrap the value in a use ref hook, the object remains stable.

17
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
To access this stable instance and update the underlying number, you simply unwrap the reference using the .current property.

18
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:05.000
However, you must never destructure these animated values to extract specific coordinates, like X and Y from a pan gesture.

19
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Doing so severs the internal proxy that tracks dependencies, breaking the value entirely.

20
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:21.000
If you fail to persist the animated value correctly across renders, you destroy the frameworks internal tracking and break its connection to the native driver.

21
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
The configuration setting usenative driver.true physically routes the animation loop away from JavaScript and onto the UI thread.

22
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Engineers frequently assume this setting is a universal fix that can smoothly animate any CSS style property.

23
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:45.000
It is not. Notice how nodes for transform and opacity properties flow synchronously into a green native GPU driver node.

24
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.000
The GPU hardware accelerates them directly, scaling and fading without recalculating physical positions.

25
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:04.000
But when nodes for layout properties, like with height and margin, attempt to flow into the GPU node, they hit a red yoga layout engine block and are violently redirected back to the JavaScript thread.

26
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Properties that alter dimensions or spacing force the yoga engine to recalculate the entire layout tree on every single frame.

27
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Color interpolation, including background color and text color, operates under a similar limitation, calculating the intermediate steps between hex codes or RGB values inherently requires JavaScript execution.

28
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:36.000
This table cleanly categorizes the divide, transform and opacity fall entirely under native support while dimensions spacing and color force a layout or JS pass.

29
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Bypassing the native requirement means the JS thread manually recalculates and dispatches values every frame. Any CPU contention immediately causes dropped frames and stuttering.

30
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Understanding the yoga layout boundary dictates whether you engineer a smooth 60 frames per second interaction or cause a jittery UI failure.

31
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
With the state stabilized and routed, you need a mechanism to drive the animated dot value forward through time.

32
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
The API provides three core animation builders to handle this mathematical progression, timing, spring and decay.

33
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.000
The animated dot timing builder advances a node from 0 to 1 over a fixed time limit.

34
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Conversely, animated dot spring is choose fixed durations, oscillating and gradually dampening around a center line until settling.

35
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Because it mimics real world physics, spring relies completely on paired parameters, configuring either friction and tension or stiffness and damping.

36
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Animated dot decay drives momentum release interactions. It accepts a high initial velocity and calculates a steep deceleration curve until the value naturally comes to rest.

37
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
These builders output raw numbers. To map those numbers into usable UI data, you chain them into an interpolate function.

38
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:57.000
The interpolation maps your input ranges to specific output ranges, translate numeric inputs into rotation degrees, strings of pixel units or precise color hex codes.

39
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:05.000
The builders strictly dictate how a value moves through time, while interpolation dictates what that movement represents on screen.

40
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Complex UI interactions require orchestrating multiple animated values simultaneously.

41
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.000
The API provides specific compositional wrappers for this orchestration.

42
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
It executes end to end, parallel runs simultaneously and stagger fires with cascading delays.

43
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Rapping an animation in a loop function with reset before iteration determines if a value snaps back to the start or continues fluidly from its resting position.

44
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:40.000
A standard UI component shell naturally rejects incoming animated data streams. You must wrap it in an animated dot create animated component shell to successfully absorb the data.

45
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:51.000
When applying this wrapper to functional react components, you must utilize forward ref. It acts as a direct conduit allowing the native driver to manipulate the underlying view outside the standard react render cycle.

46
00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Without forward ref, the native driver cannot bypass react to update the UI directly, which renders the entire animation graph useless.

47
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.000
This graph architecture is highly efficient for predefined animations, but it excels when driven by continuous high frequency user input like scroll tracking.

48
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Using animated dot event, you can map nested values from a native touch or scroll event directly into an animated dot value.

49
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:30.000
This diagram shows a mobile interface with a scrolling list. The raw y-axis scroll output feeds instantly through an interpolation graph, seamlessly adjusting the opacity and translation of the sticky header above.

50
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:42.000
You control the flow of these events using scroll event throttle. A value of 16 limits dispatches to roughly one per frame, balancing the reduction of JavaScript events while minimizing lag in any derivative functions.

51
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Nothing user events directly on the UI thread demonstrates the power of the new architecture, handling heavy, continuous interaction with absolutely zero JavaScript overhead.

52
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.000
Even with the architecture properly aligned, a few strict runtime constraints often catch engineers off guard.

53
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.000
First, all animations nested inside a parallel composition must use the exact same driver configuration.

54
00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:13.000
Mixing native and non-native drivers throws a hard runtime error in react native 0.7 cents.

55
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:21.000
Second, attempting to read a dot current animated value directly during a component render cycle is a severe anti-pattern that breaks synchronization.

56
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Finally, if you execute a looping animation where the initial and final resting values are mathematically identical, you must manually trigger a dot set value reset before the next iteration.

57
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:42.000
By treating the animated API as a declarative graph builder rather than an imperative executor, you fully leverage JSI to match native performance without compromise.

